// BPT midterm: HARD MCQ (HQ_1), open questions (OQ_1) and extra normal MCQ (NQ_1) for pages fw, buit, hall, marx
const HQ_1=[
/* ---- fw ---- */
{"t":"fw","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rydin (2021) presents her book as analytic theory OF planning rather than normative planning theory FOR planning. Statement II: Rydin holds that analytic research, if done rigorously enough, can be kept free of value judgements.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: she explicitly situates the book in the analytic approach (theory of planning, after Faludi). II is false: she immediately adds that normative and analytic are 'closely entangled' and that 'all research, including analytic research, is imbued with value judgements'. The tempting reading 'both true' confuses being analytic with being value-free."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rydin rejects the natural-science view of theory as a law connecting variables as a model for planning research. Statement II: The social world is complex and interacts with built and natural environments, and social scientists interpret how people themselves interpret the world.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and II is exactly Rydin's reason (following Flyvbjerg et al.): complexity plus Giddens' double hermeneutic make the natural sciences a poor model, so she adopts theory as a heuristic aid instead. 'Both true, but no explanation' misses that II is the stated ground for I."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"combo","q":"Which of these are objections Rydin raises against empiricism? I. Deciding which data to collect, and how, already involves value judgements. II. The same data allow alternative interpretations, and choosing one again involves values. III. Empiricism overreaches by turning case findings into law-like generalisations.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are her two pragmatic objections. III inverts her third point: she says pure empiricism produces a 'disparate field of knowledge' in which findings are hard to connect, i.e. too little generalisation, not too much. That is why theory's 'link to a broader story' is presented as a remedy."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a PhD researcher joins a Rotterdam tenants' association fighting a demolition plan, helps them prepare objections and studies the dispute from the inside. She says her work is phronetic and needs no theoretical framework. How would Rydin (2021) respond?","o":["Her praxis already assumes planning is a political process riven with conflict: a hidden theoretical choice","Her praxis already assumes change in planning comes through feedback and communicative action with political leaders","Her research is normative planning theory, so it falls outside analytic planning research altogether","Her research is empiricist, since it lets the facts of the dispute speak for themselves without filters"],"a":0,"e":"Rydin's second objection to phronesis is that its praxis hides theory: helping community groups in planning disputes implies a view of planning as a political process 'riven with conflict' (urban politics, ch. 6). The feedback/communicative-action assumption is the other example, which she links to working with political leaders and chief executives, not with a protest group."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Rydin's (2021) view of theory most precisely?","o":["A heuristic aid that, used rigorously, yields a complete explanation of a planning situation","A set of tested laws linking variables, used heuristically when good data are scarce","An arbitrary window on the world, chosen for convenience rather than for any conviction","A heuristic aid that helps us understand a situation that can never be known completely"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin calls theory 'a heuristic aid to understanding a situation, a way to try to know what is essentially unknowable in its completeness'. The 'complete explanation' paraphrase overstates, the 'tested laws' paraphrase reintroduces the law model she rejects, and the 'arbitrary window' paraphrase contradicts her claim that theories are 'not just framings in the sense of an arbitrary selection of a window'."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"critique","q":"Rydin (2021) treats theory as a partial, value-laden narrative that researchers should choose consciously. Which criticism fits the logic of the perspective it is attributed to?","o":["A Marxist political economist: Rydin gives too much weight to structural economic forces and too little to individual choice","A Hayekian liberal: Rydin's pluralism of theories places far too much faith in dispersed, decentralised local knowledge of places","An advocacy planner (Davidoff): making value judgements explicit drags research too far into politics and goal-setting","A 1960s systems planner: treating theory as a value-laden narrative gives up the search for scientific, testable general laws"],"a":3,"e":"The systems planners saw 'planning as a science, not an art' and assumed universal, monist knowledge, so they would object to theory as heuristic narrative. A Marxist would criticise too little attention to structure, not too much; Hayek celebrates dispersed knowledge; and Davidoff wanted goal-setting made explicit and political."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Rydin's first advantage of using theory?","o":["Frameworks remove value judgements from research, so data collection becomes neutral, so a time- and money-limited project can collect all relevant data on its topic","Frameworks differ on what matters most, so a chosen theory makes these judgements explicit, so a limited project can focus on some data and downplay others","Frameworks link a case to a broader story, so findings become complete and final, so the theory needs no further revision after the project","Frameworks let patterns emerge from rigorously collected data, so theory can be confirmed as law, so research becomes cumulative and objective"],"a":1,"e":"The first advantage is focus: theory makes explicit what is important so the researcher can concentrate effort within practical limits. The 'neutral data collection' chain breaks at 'remove value judgements' (theory makes them explicit, it does not remove them); the 'broader story' chain breaks at 'complete and final', since Rydin calls empirical accounts 'partial and provisional' and wants research to revise theory."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with the HC1 lecture on normative commitments?","o":["Evaluative claims ask which planning processes are fair and which outcomes should be strived for","Explanatory claims are where normative commitments enter most directly, since they say why outcomes occur","Normative commitments shape what we see as a problem and therefore what research should measure","In the Metropolis case, the chosen commitment decides which criterion is used to compare the two housing plans"],"a":1,"e":"The lecture highlighted the evaluative ('are fair', 'should be strive[n] for') and prescriptive ('should good planners') wording as the place where normativity enters; explanatory claims (how planning works, why outcomes occur) were not highlighted. The other three statements match the slides, including the concluding slide on what normative commitments shape."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"who","q":"Which author would endorse this claim? 'Defining planning as future-oriented processes through which actors try to achieve control takes planners' intentions at face value and aligns research with the professed values of practice.'","o":["Næss and Saglie (2000)","Rydin (2021)","Hall (2014)","Buitelaar (2026)"],"a":1,"e":"This is Rydin's critique of Næss and Saglie's definition: by privileging instrumental rationality they take planners' intentions at face value. Næss and Saglie are the most tempting option because the definition is theirs, but they propose it; they do not criticise it."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a researcher argues that Amsterdam's housing pattern results from countless uncoordinated decisions by households, investors and the municipality, none of whom intended the overall outcome. Where does this claim sit most precisely on the course framework?","o":["Dimension 1, agency: the outcome is explained by the intentional choices of individual actors","Dimension 2a, transformation: the outcome shows existing arrangements being fundamentally changed","Dimension 2b, emergent: the outcome arises from interacting actions that no one designed","Dimension 3b, pluralist: many actors each hold valid and relevant knowledge about the outcome"],"a":2,"e":"The key point is that nobody intended the result: stability and change 'emerging from interacting actions and wider processes' is the emergent pole of dimension 2b. The agency option is tempting because actors decide, but dimension 1 asks how much weight individual action gets as a source of outcomes, not whether the overall pattern was designed."},
/* ---- buit ---- */
{"t":"buit","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Buitelaar classifies the material Equal City as goal-based. Statement II: Material egalitarianism aims at a preferred end-state distribution rather than at securing individual rights.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and II is the reason for I: because material egalitarianism aims at a preferred end-state, it is goal-based, like the Happy City. Buitelaar adds that egalitarians who stress equal rights (formal equality) are rights-based instead, which confirms that the end-state orientation is what makes the material version goal-based."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Buitelaar classifies the Supportive City as rights-based. Statement II: Sen notes that a lower standard of living proves inequality, but not poverty unless the actual standard is known.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both statements are true, but II does not explain I. The Supportive City is rights-based because it cares about a minimum that everyone is entitled to, not about the pattern above it; Sen's remark is an argument against equality as a goal (the sufficientarian critique of egalitarianism), not the reason for its rights-based basis."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"combo","q":"Which combinations does Buitelaar treat as congruent (workable)? I. The Free City's rights-based approach with the Supportive City's primary goods. II. Capabilities with a sufficiency threshold. III. Individual rights with the utilitarian principle of maximum collective utility.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II work: primary goods can be institutional rights and freedoms (they are in Rawls' list), and capabilities fit sufficiency because they have an absolutist core. III is his example of internal incongruence: individual rights clash with the utilitarian principle of collective welfare."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: two Utrecht households both spend 35% of their income on rent. One earns EUR 6,000 a month, the other EUR 1,800. A policy officer wants an affordability measure that separates the household at real risk from the one that is not. What does Buitelaar's discussion point to?","o":["The housing ratio: housing costs as a share of household income, with 30% as the usual upper limit","The budget approach: check whether income after housing costs covers a basic household budget","The room stress factor: rooms available divided by rooms required, minus one","Hedonic price analysis: comparing the prices of exposed and unexposed properties"],"a":1,"e":"Both households score the same on the housing ratio, which Buitelaar says is simple but says little about payment risk without absolute income; the budget approach solves exactly this. The room stress factor measures adequacy (space), not affordability, and hedonic pricing belongs to the Happy City's valuation of externalities."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch city demolishes part of a large social-housing estate and sells plots to middle-income buyers, arguing that concentrations of poor households harm residents' life chances. In Buitelaar's terms, what justification is this, and what risk does he flag?","o":["An intrinsic Equal City ground (inequality is unjust in itself); risk of losing agglomeration gains in the city","A Supportive City ground (absolute poverty matters); risk of setting the threshold too low","An instrumental Equal City ground (neighbourhood effects); risk of displacement and stigmatisation","A Happy City ground (maximum collective utility); risk of ignoring individual property rights"],"a":2,"e":"Arguing that segregation harms individuals through negative neighbourhood effects is the instrumental ground of the Equal City, and social (re)mixing is its spatial policy; Buitelaar notes such targeting can cause displacement and stigmatisation of people and places. The intrinsic ground would say inequality is wrong in itself, regardless of effects on life chances."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"critique","q":"Buitelaar argues that the Free City accepts whatever pattern emerges from just rules, since 'liberty upsets designed patterns'. Which criticism fits the logic of the perspective it is attributed to?","o":["Utilitarian: the Free City wrongly puts maximum collective welfare above the rights and freedoms of individuals","Sufficientarian: the Free City fails because it does not make everyone's income and wealth exactly the same","Marxist: equal formal rights under private property mask class inequality, so free settlement reproduces it","Hayekian: the Free City relies far too much on designed order and too little on spontaneous, self-organised order"],"a":2,"e":"For Marx formal freedom under private property is servitude, and outcomes follow from the structure of ownership, so equal rights reproduce class division. A utilitarian would complain of the reverse (rights blocking welfare maximisation), a sufficientarian rejects equality as a goal, and the Free City is itself built on Hayek's spontaneous order."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Buitelaar's central argument most precisely?","o":["Pursuing The Just City requires choosing exactly one conception, because all four conceptions are mutually incompatible","Pursuing The Just City requires finding common ground between conceptions quickly, to avoid conflict over values","Pursuing The Just City requires explicit choices between conceptions, since some lead to different, incompatible policies","Pursuing The Just City requires rejecting neoliberal conceptions, since justice is a reaction to market-led policy"],"a":2,"e":"Buitelaar wants explicit choices and 'justice literacy' because conceptions lead to different policies and some are incongruent. The 'exactly one' paraphrase overstates: he shows Free and Supportive can combine. Rushing to common ground is what he warns against, and the anti-neoliberal framing is Fainstein's narrow conception that he criticises."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"who","q":"Which author would endorse this claim? 'Social justice is a meaningless idea in a spontaneous order, because no designer exists who can be held responsible for the resulting distribution.'","o":["Marx (1844)","Hayek (1976)","Madden and Marcuse (2016)","Rydin (2021)"],"a":1,"e":"Buitelaar draws this argument from Hayek (1976): in a spontaneous order (cosmos) there is no 'divider', so justice applies only to individual conduct and rules. Marx and Madden and Marcuse hold the opposite: distributions are produced by the structure of property relations and should be changed."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about Buitelaar's 'materials of justice' is NOT accurate?","o":["Scope gets little attention in the article because it does not differ between the four conceptions he discusses","Basis separates goal-based conceptions from conceptions that are rights- or duty-based","Ground gives the reasons for a conception, which can be instrumental or deontological","The principle specifies who or where justice applies to, for instance future generations or non-humans"],"a":3,"e":"Who or where justice applies to is the scope, not the principle; the principle states how values are justly allocated (e.g. maximum collective utility, everyone enough, assigning rights). The statement about scope getting little attention is true, which makes it the tempting but wrong pick."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs why, for Buitelaar, the Free City cannot also correct segregation?","o":["Free City grants freedom to settle, move and trade homes, so people spread evenly across the city, so segregation disappears without any planning policy","Free City grants freedom to settle, move and trade homes, so people cluster with similar people, so targeted subsidies can fix segregation while leaving all rights intact","Free City grants freedom to settle, move and trade homes, so prices allocate homes efficiently, so a Pareto optimum also guarantees an equal social spread","Free City grants freedom to settle, move and trade homes, so people cluster with similar people, so correcting segregation means limiting those freedoms for some"],"a":3,"e":"Buitelaar: with liberty, an even spread is unlikely ('birds of a feather flock together'), and correcting the pattern requires 'reducing or eliminating rights for some people to settle freely, move freely, and to trade homes freely', which is formal inequality. The subsidy chain is tempting but breaks at the last link: correcting the pattern implies limiting rights."},
/* ---- hall ---- */
{"t":"hall","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Hall argues that the 1960s systems planners shared some metaphysical assumptions with the blueprint planners before them. Statement II: Both treated the planning system as active, the city as passive and politics as benign and receptive to expert advice.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and II spells out what the shared assumptions were, so it explains I. This is part of Hall's argument that the systems revolution was less complete than claimed: beneath the new science lay the old view of an expert acting on a passive city."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Hall calls the Marxian logic 'strangely quietist'. Statement II: Marxist planning theorists such as Scott and Roweis consistently refused to tell progressive planners what to do.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is true. II is false: Hall's point is that Scott and Roweis denied that theory can assume transcendent norms, yet also said a viable theory should tell us 'what we can, and must, do as progressive planners', which he calls 'sheer rhetoric'. Quietism follows from the logic (planners cannot divert capitalism by more than 'a millimeter or a millisecond'), not from a consistent refusal to prescribe."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"combo","q":"Following Nigel Taylor, which of these were elements of the 1960s shift in planning thought that Hall describes? I. Towns seen as systems of interrelated activities in constant flux. II. Greater emphasis on the aesthetic quality of physical urban form. III. Planning as a continuous process rather than the production of end-state plans.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and III are among Taylor's four elements (with a socio-economic view and scientific skills). II is the reverse of the shift: the new view was socio-economic rather than aesthetic, since 'planning was a science, not an art'."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a regional authority publishes a land-use and transport model forecasting congestion around a new station. Once published, the forecast changes the decisions of councillors, developers and commuters, so the model keeps being overtaken by the reactions it provokes. Which point in Hall fits this most precisely?","o":["Lindblom's claim that real decisions proceed by muddling through, in small steps of bargaining between groups","The logical paradox that the urban system sits inside its own system of control, unlike a weapons system","The Marxist claim that planning always solves one problem only by creating another for capital","Rittel and Webber's view that planning problems are wicked because they have no stopping rule at all"],"a":1,"e":"Hall's 'core problem' is that in a weapons system the controls are inside the system, but the urban-regional system is 'inside its own system of control': the controller is part of what it tries to steer. Muddling through is tempting but describes how decisions are made, not why a model of the city cannot stand outside the city."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a tenants' association in Amsterdam hires a planner to draft its own alternative plan for its neighbourhood and to argue it at council hearings against the municipality's plan. Which approach in Hall's chapter does this fit most precisely?","o":["Forester's communicative planning: planners foster undistorted dialogue among all affected parties","Lindblom's incrementalism: policy advances in small bargained steps between existing interest groups","Friedmann's social learning: decisions devolve to small groups learning by doing in their own setting","Davidoff's advocacy planning: planners represent particular groups and make competing plans explicit"],"a":3,"e":"Advocacy planning has planners working for specific groups, making goal-setting explicit and forcing agencies to compete with alternative plans. Communicative planning is the tempting option, but it aims at revealing rather than distorting communication among all parties, not at the planner acting as one side's advocate."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"critique","q":"Hall is sympathetic to the 1950s and 1960s alliance of science and practice and wants theory that helps 'the boring job of planning real places'. Which criticism of Hall fits the logic of the perspective it is attributed to?","o":["Lindblom-style pluralist: Hall underrates how much comprehensive, optimising analysis can achieve in real-world politics","1960s systems planner: Hall overstates how far the city can be modelled, monitored and controlled by experts","Marxist: wanting useful theory ignores that planning theory is itself a product of the forces that create planning","Postmodernist: Hall is too relativist about what can be known with certainty about cities and their futures"],"a":2,"e":"Marxists argue that 'planning theory is nothing other than a creation of the social forces that bring planning into existence', so a plea for practically useful theory misses what theory is. Pluralists reject comprehensive optimisation, systems planners would defend modelling and control, and postmodernists are the relativists Hall attacks."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Hall's verdict on Forester's communicative planning most precisely?","o":["Forester's theory is philosophically empty, so its prescriptions are useless for anyone doing practical, day-to-day planning work","Forester's prescription is a radical break with all earlier planning, because it is grounded in Habermas's theory","Forester's theory revives the systems approach by treating planning communication as a feedback and control loop","Without its philosophical basis, the practical prescription is democratic common sense, much like Davidoff's earlier advocacy"],"a":3,"e":"Hall says that, stripped of its 'Germanic philosophical basis', the prescription 'all comes out as good old-fashioned democratic common sense, no more and no less than Davidoff's advocacy planning of 15 years before'. He does not call it useless (that overstates), and his point is precisely that it is not a radical break."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs the mid-1970s 'paradigm crisis' that Hall describes?","o":["Rational planning merged process and substance, so theory focused on the physical design of cities, so procedural theory of deciding was pushed to the margins","Rational planning separated process from substance, so theory focused on how to decide whatever is planned, so substantive theory of cities was pushed to the margins","Rational planning separated process from substance, so theory focused on how to decide whatever is planned, so planners quickly regained their old priestly expert mystique","Rational planning separated process from substance, so theory focused on physical form and aesthetics, so substantive theory of cities was pushed to the margins"],"a":1,"e":"Planning as 'a special way of deciding and acting' independent of what is planned (Webber, Faludi) separated process from substance; by the mid-1970s this had pushed substantive theory to the margins. The 'regained mystique' chain breaks at the last link: in this period planners lost their 'priestly clothing'."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A municipal planning report argues: 'We will not fix one masterplan for 2040. Instead we will continuously monitor traffic, population and land use, model how the city's interacting subsystems affect each other, forecast alternatives and adjust our programmes as new information arrives.' Which approach in Hall grounds this passage, and why?","o":["1960s systems and process planning: the city as interacting subsystems, monitored and steered continuously","Lindblom's incrementalism: decisions in small, remedial steps because no one has comprehensive knowledge","Pre-1955 craft planning: survey, analysis and design produce a plan that the expert himself then adjusts over time","Communicative planning: programmes are adapted as stakeholders reach shared understanding in dialogue"],"a":0,"e":"Monitoring, modelling interacting subsystems and adapting programmes during implementation is the systems view (McLoughlin, cybernetics) combined with Faludi's process planning. Incrementalism is tempting because programmes are adjusted, but Lindblom rejects exactly this comprehensive modelling ambition; craft planning produced a fixed, single-shot plan."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about pre-1955 planning, as Hall presents it, is NOT accurate?","o":["Goals were mostly implicit and were derived intuitively from the planners' own 'expert' and apolitical values","The process was single-shot: survey, then analysis, then directly design of the plan","Batty calls it a golden age in which the planner would 'get it right first time'","Implementation and updating were central, since the 1947 Act required continuous monitoring of the plan"],"a":3,"e":"Hall says implementation and updating were barely discussed; the 1947 Act required five-yearly updates but still assumed a fixed land-use plan, so continuous monitoring belongs to the 1960s systems view. The other three statements are accurate descriptions of the craft era."},
/* ---- marx ---- */
{"t":"marx","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: In 'Estranged Labour' Marx treats private property as the starting point that explains estranged labour. Statement II: Marx complains that political economy starts from the fact of private property without explaining it.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"I is false: Marx reverses political economy, arguing that private property is 'the product, the result, the necessary consequence, of alienated labor' (only later does the relation become reciprocal). II is true and is exactly why he refuses to start where political economy starts."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: For Marx, estrangement is not only a private experience of the worker but also a social relation between people. Statement II: The estrangement of man from man follows as an immediate consequence of estrangement from the product, from life activity and from species-being.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and II explains I: because each person relates to others from the estranged position in which he finds himself as a worker, estrangement from product, activity and species-being becomes estrangement between people. That fourth form is what makes alienation a social relation."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about species-being match 'Estranged Labour'? I. Estranged labour makes species-life the purpose of life and physical existence merely its means. II. Humans make their life activity itself an object of their will and consciousness. III. The animal produces only under immediate physical need, whereas man also produces when free from it.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"II and III are Marx's account of what distinguishes conscious human life activity. I reverses his point: estranged labour 'makes man's species-life a means to his physical existence', i.e. what makes us human becomes a mere means of survival."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a meal-delivery courier in Utrecht says: 'I don't care what I deliver. What kills me is that the app dictates every minute and every route; I only feel like myself after my shift.' Which form of estrangement does he describe most precisely?","o":["Estrangement from the product: what he produces confronts him as an alien power that dominates him","Estrangement from the act of production: labour is external, coerced, and he feels at home only outside it","Estrangement from species-being: his free, conscious activity becomes merely a means to his physical survival","Estrangement from other people: he relates to others only from his position as an estranged worker"],"a":1,"e":"He explicitly does not care about the product; his complaint is about the activity itself, which Marx describes as external and forced: 'He feels at home when he is not working, and when he is working he does not feel at home.' Species-being is a tempting choice, but the case is about feeling outside oneself during work, which is Marx's second aspect, self-estrangement."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: construction workers build luxury apartments on the Amsterdam waterfront. The more value their work adds to the city's property market, the higher the rents that push them out of the city. Which idea from Marx fits this most precisely?","o":["Estrangement from the act of production: the work is coerced, and the workers feel at home only outside of it","Estrangement from species-being: building is reduced from free creative activity to a means of survival","Estrangement from other people: workers relate to residents only as owners and propertyless strangers","Estrangement from the product: the more wealth workers produce, the more it confronts them as an alien power"],"a":3,"e":"The case turns on the result of labour turning against its producers: 'The worker becomes all the poorer the more wealth he produces', and the product confronts him 'as something alien, as a power independent of the producer'. Nothing in the case concerns how the work itself is experienced, which rules out the act-of-production reading."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"critique","q":"Marx's text implies that emancipation requires ending private property, since higher wages cannot restore human dignity. Which criticism fits the logic of the perspective it is attributed to?","o":["Hayek: abolishing private property concentrates economic power in the state and destroys the dispersed decisions freedom depends on","Buitelaar's Supportive City: Marx cares too much about making sure everyone has enough and far too little about equality between people","Rydin's political economy chapter: Marx's account is too actor-centred and ignores the structural economic forces shaping action","Buitelaar's Happy City: Marx's focus on maximising total collective welfare neglects the individual rights of property owners"],"a":0,"e":"Hayek defends private property and competition as conditions of freedom and decentralised knowledge, so he would attack abolition on exactly these grounds. A sufficientarian would object that Marx cares too much about equality, Rydin classifies political economy as structural, and maximising collective welfare is the utilitarian position, not Marx's."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase states Marx's view of the relation between private property and alienated labour most precisely?","o":["Private property is the product of alienated labour, though it later also becomes the means by which labour alienates itself","Private property is the original cause of alienated labour, which is why political economy rightly starts from it","Private property is the product of alienated labour and it has no further effect on labour or the worker once it has come into being","Private property is unrelated to alienated labour, which stems entirely from the technical division of labour"],"a":0,"e":"Marx calls private property 'the product, the result, the necessary consequence, of alienated labor', and adds that the relation later becomes reciprocal: it is both product and means of alienation. The 'no further effect' paraphrase understates by dropping this reciprocity; the 'original cause' paraphrase is the political-economy view he rejects."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs Marx's argument that the worker grows poorer as wealth grows?","o":["The product of labour belongs to another, so each product raises the market value of labour, so the worker's wages and dignity rise as he produces more","The product of labour belongs to the worker, so each product strengthens the alien power of capital, so the worker becomes poorer and more dominated as he produces more","The product of labour belongs to another, so each product strengthens the alien power of capital, so the worker becomes poorer and more dominated as he produces more","The product of labour belongs to another, so each product strengthens the alien power of capital, so the worker gains status by consuming more of the goods he produces"],"a":2,"e":"The more the worker produces, 'the less he can possess and the more he falls under the dominion of his product, capital': the world of things gains value as the world of men loses it. The wages-and-dignity chain breaks at its middle link; Marx says the worker becomes 'an ever cheaper commodity the more commodities he creates'."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"who","q":"Which author would endorse this claim? 'Housing problems are not the result of greed or dishonesty. They result from the structural logic of the current housing system.'","o":["Glaeser and Gyourko (2003)","Hayek (1944)","Madden and Marcuse (2016)","Buitelaar (2026)"],"a":2,"e":"The HC4 slide quotes Madden and Marcuse, the progressive (Marxist) planners: housing problems are structural, so the answer is decommodified housing. Glaeser and Gyourko, the liberal counterpart on the same slide, blame distortions from land-use regulation, not the market's structural logic."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A housing article argues: 'Raising housing allowances will not solve the crisis. As long as homes are built and traded for their exchange value, those who need housing most will never register as demand. Only taking land and housing out of the market, through public and cooperative ownership, addresses the problem at its root.' Which perspective grounds this passage, and why?","o":["Buitelaar's Supportive City: everyone needs enough housing, so allowances should be raised until all reach an adequate threshold","Hayek's liberalism: allowances distort prices, so land and housing should be left entirely to free exchange","Buitelaar's Happy City: housing should go where willingness to pay is highest to maximise collective welfare","Marxist political economy (Madden and Marcuse): unaffordability is how the market works, so housing must be decommodified"],"a":3,"e":"Rejecting better payments within the market (like Marx's 'better payment for the slave'), distinguishing need from effective demand and calling for public and cooperative ownership is Madden and Marcuse's decommodification argument. The Supportive City is tempting because it is about enough housing, but it would accept allowances as a way to reach the threshold, which the passage rejects."}
];

const OQ_1=[
/* ---- fw ---- */
{"t":"fw","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain why Rydin (2021) rejects empiricism ('let the facts speak for themselves') and what she says theory offers instead.","model":"Empiricism, which Latour calls the 'modernist settlement', assumes the world is 'out there' and reveals itself through rigorous data collection. Rydin's objections are pragmatic. First, the supposedly neutral practice is full of value judgements: you cannot collect all data, so why this data, collected this way, on a topic defined this way? Second, data allow alternative interpretations, and choosing one again involves values (her housebuilding-rates example). Third, pure empiricism yields a disparate field in which findings are hard to connect. Theory answers these problems: it focuses attention under limited time and money, links partial findings to a broader story (allowing generalisation), and lets empirical research build and revise theory.","rubric":["Defines empiricism as letting facts speak for themselves (optionally Latour's modernist settlement)","Names value judgements in data selection and/or interpretation","Names the problem of a disparate, unconnected field","States at least two of the three advantages of theory (focus, broader narrative/generalisation, theory building)"]},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"A master's thesis proposal on Dutch housing targets argues: 'Planning is the process through which the municipality tries to achieve control over the future in order to implement its intentions. We take the council's housing targets as given and do not question them. We will collect all available data on completions and permits and let the figures speak for themselves; no theoretical framework is needed, since the numbers will show which instrument works best.' (a) Which view of planning and of theory grounds this passage? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the passage, and indicate how Rydin (2021) would assess them. (10)","model":"(a) The passage combines an instrumental, governmental definition of planning (close to Næss and Saglie's 'future-oriented processes through which the actors attempt to achieve control') with an empiricist view of research that denies the need for theory. (b) Argument 1, instrumental framing: planning is 'the process through which the municipality tries to achieve control over the future in order to implement its intentions' and the targets are taken 'as given'. Rydin argues that any definition of planning 'implies a framing of that object of research and that has theoretical implications'; this framing privileges instrumental rationality and takes planners' intentions at face value, aligning the research with practice's professed values, so it is itself a value judgement. Argument 2, empiricism: 'let the figures speak for themselves; no theoretical framework is needed'. Rydin rejects this: it is impossible to collect 'all available data' without choices about what to collect and how, the same data can support different narratives (her housebuilding-rates example), and pure empiricism leaves a disparate field. A theory would make these choices explicit, focus the project and link it to a broader story. Even 'which instrument works best' presupposes a criterion of 'best', which is a normative commitment. A stronger proposal would state its definition of planning and its theoretical lens openly, so that readers can see which value judgements frame the research.","rubric":["(a) Identifies an instrumental/governmental definition of planning AND an empiricist view of theory","Argument 1 quotes the control/intentions or 'as given' phrase and links it to Rydin's point that defining planning is a theoretical, value-laden choice","Argument 2 quotes 'speak for themselves' or 'no theoretical framework is needed' and gives Rydin's objections to empiricism","Notes that 'works best' already implies a normative commitment or that theory would focus the research","Quotes are taken verbatim from the passage"]},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"position","pts":10,"q":"Place Rydin's own view of what theory is on the course's knowledge dimension (universal vs situated; monist vs pluralist; realism vs relativism). Justify your placement with the text.","model":"Rydin sits on the situated and pluralist side, but not at the relativist extreme. Situated: she rejects the natural-science model of theory as a law connecting variables, following Flyvbjerg et al., because of the social world's complexity and Giddens' double hermeneutic. Theory is instead 'a heuristic aid to understanding a situation, a way to try to know what is essentially unknowable in its completeness': knowledge is partial and built by layering analyses. Pluralist: planning research legitimately draws on many theories, each with its own ontology, normative position and epistemology, and the 'terrain of theory is, therefore, a contested one'; she gives no single theory priority and says researchers 'have a right to reflect their own value judgements'. Yet she is not a relativist: theories are 'not just framings in the sense of an arbitrary selection of a window', persuasiveness matters, and research should make a 'contribution to knowledge' about the causes of things. This middle position fits her 2007 argument that multiple knowledges exist but claims can be tested.","rubric":["Places Rydin towards situated/pluralist with a clear statement","Uses the rejection of law-like theory (complexity, double hermeneutic) as evidence","Uses the heuristic definition and/or contested terrain of theory as evidence","Explains why she is not fully relativist (not arbitrary, persuasiveness, contribution to knowledge)","Faithful to the text, with at least one accurate quote or close paraphrase"]},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"Use the HC1 Metropolis case (2,000 market-rate homes, rule-compliant and without subsidy, versus 1,600 homes with 40% affordable, priority for low-income households and a EUR 50 million municipal contribution). Show how explanatory, evaluative and prescriptive claims differ in this case, and explain why the lecture says normative commitments shape what we measure.","model":"An explanatory claim would say how and why outcomes arise, for example: 'the market plan produces homes that most low-income households cannot afford because developers build for effective demand'. An evaluative claim judges the plans: 'the mixed plan is fairer because 40% affordability reaches those in unaffordable or inadequate housing'. A prescriptive claim tells the planner what to do: 'the municipality should require the mixed development'. The lecture highlighted the evaluative and prescriptive questions as the place where normative commitments, 'the underlying values and principles used to evaluate planning', enter. Commitments shape what we see as a problem, what planning should achieve and how outcomes are judged, hence what we measure. A Free City view measures whether rights and rules were respected and favours the rule-compliant market plan; a Happy City view counts total homes and welfare per euro; a Supportive City view measures how many of the 2,000 low-income households end up adequately and affordably housed and favours the mixed plan. The 'better' plan therefore depends on the commitment chosen, which is why it should be explicit.","rubric":["Gives a correct example of each claim type (explanatory, evaluative, prescriptive) applied to the case","States that normativity enters mainly through evaluative and prescriptive claims","Lists what normative commitments shape (problem, aims, evaluation/measurement)","Shows at least two commitments leading to different measures or a different preferred plan","Concludes that the choice of commitment should be explicit"]},
/* ---- buit ---- */
{"t":"buit","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain Buitelaar's distinction between the concept and conceptions of justice, and why it matters for what he calls 'justice literacy'.","model":"Following Moroni (after Rawls), the concept of justice is the general idea that almost everyone supports ('few people would oppose the idea of a just city'); a conception is a specific version of it, with its own values, principles, grounds and basis. Buitelaar distinguishes four dominant conceptions: the Happy (utilitarian), Equal (egalitarian), Supportive (sufficientarian) and Free (liberal) City. The distinction matters because agreement on the concept hides disagreement on conceptions: vagueness creates superficial consensus, narrow definitions (e.g. Fainstein's anti-neoliberal framing) present one conception as the concept, and pursuing incompatible conceptions at once leads to inconsistency. Justice literacy means knowing the vocabulary (the conceptions) and the grammar (how they connect), so choices become explicit.","rubric":["Correctly defines concept vs conception","Names at least two of the four conceptions as examples","Explains at least one of the three problems (vagueness, narrowness, inconsistency)","Defines justice literacy as vocabulary plus grammar"]},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"A municipal housing vision states: 'Our goal is not to close the gap between rich and poor. What matters is that every household can live in an adequate home it can afford. We therefore set a clear minimum: no household should live in overcrowded conditions or be left, after paying rent, without enough money for a basic household budget. Households furthest below this line get help first. Above it, differences in housing are not the city's concern.' (a) Which conception of justice grounds this vision? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the passage. (10)","model":"(a) The Supportive City (sufficientarianism) in Buitelaar's terms, in its prioritarian variant. (b) Argument 1, sufficiency rather than equality: 'Our goal is not to close the gap between rich and poor. What matters is that every household can live in an adequate home it can afford.' This echoes Frankfurt's line that what matters 'is not that everyone should have the same but that each should have enough': absolute rather than relative poverty, which distinguishes it from the Equal City. 'Above it, differences in housing are not the city's concern' confirms that the pattern above the minimum is irrelevant, which is why Buitelaar calls this conception rights-based. Argument 2, a measurable threshold: 'no household should live in overcrowded conditions or be left, after paying rent, without enough money for a basic household budget'. Policy in the Supportive City centres on setting and measuring the threshold: overcrowding corresponds to adequacy measures such as the room stress factor, and the after-rent test is the budget approach that Buitelaar prefers to the simple 30% housing ratio. 'Households furthest below this line get help first' adds prioritarianism (priority to the worst off below the threshold). Because the vision cares about a minimum and not the pattern, it can be combined with a Free City framework of rights, but it would sit uneasily with an Equal City goal of social mixing.","rubric":["(a) Names the Supportive City / sufficientarianism","Argument 1 quotes the 'not to close the gap' or 'not the city's concern' line and explains enough vs equal (absolute vs relative)","Argument 2 quotes the threshold sentence and links it to room stress and/or the budget approach","Recognises the prioritarian element ('furthest below') for full marks","Quotes are taken verbatim from the passage"]},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare Buitelaar's Free City with Marx's 'Estranged Labour' on one specific point: whether freely acquired private property and equal formal rights can produce a just situation.","model":"For the Free City, yes. Following Hayek and Nozick, justice lies in the rules, not in the resulting pattern: 'The principle is assigning rights rather than distributing outcomes.' Nozick's entitlement theory judges only acquisition and transfer, so an investor who legitimately buys many homes is no sign of injustice, and redistribution itself would create formal inequality by reducing some people's rights. Inequality is the expected result of liberty, since 'liberty upsets designed patterns'. For Marx, no. Private property is not a neutral starting point but 'the product, the result, the necessary consequence, of alienated labor'. Under private property and wage labour 'the worker becomes all the poorer the more wealth he produces', so formal freedom to sell one's labour is forced labour, and even 'an enforced increase of wages' would be 'nothing but better payment for the slave'. The difference is ontological: the Free City looks at individual conduct within rules (agency, emergent order), Marx at the structure of ownership that produces classes (structure). Buitelaar would place Marx's concerns near the Equal City, which he shows is externally incongruent with the Free City.","rubric":["States the Free City position: justice of rules/entitlements, not outcomes (Hayek/Nozick)","States Marx's position: private property as product of estranged labour; formal freedom/wages do not restore dignity","Uses at least one accurate quote or close paraphrase from each text","Identifies the underlying difference (individual conduct vs structure, or formal vs material equality)","Notes the external incongruence or Equal City link"]},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch city requires that every new development include a fixed share of social rental housing, so that each neighbourhood has a mix of incomes and enough affordable homes. Analyse this rule with Buitelaar's conceptions and his idea of congruence, and say how a Free City advocate would assess it.","model":"The rule combines two conceptions. Its mixing aim belongs to the Equal City: spreading income groups evenly to reduce segregation, justified instrumentally (neighbourhood effects) or intrinsically (inequality is wrong), with social mixing as the typical spatial policy. Its affordability aim belongs to the Supportive City: providing a spatial primary good, adequate and affordable housing, so that everyone has enough. These two can be combined on the policy level, but their grounds differ: the Supportive City is indifferent to the pattern above the threshold, the Equal City targets the pattern itself, so the city should be clear which goal decides when they conflict (e.g. whether to build social housing where need is greatest or where mixing gains are greatest). Buitelaar also warns that mixing can cause displacement and stigmatisation. A Free City advocate would reject the quota: like inclusionary zoning it restricts owners' and developers' rights to use and trade land, and correcting the social pattern requires limiting freedom to settle, which produces formal inequality. Since 'liberty upsets designed patterns', the Free City is externally incongruent with the Equal City goal of the rule.","rubric":["Identifies the Equal City (mixing) element with its ground","Identifies the Supportive City (affordable housing as primary good) element","Discusses congruence: the two can be combined but differ in goal/basis, or names a tension","Gives the Free City critique (restriction of rights, formal inequality, liberty upsets patterns)","Uses Buitelaar's terms accurately"]},
/* ---- hall ---- */
{"t":"hall","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain what Hall means when he says that between 1965 and 1975 planners were stripped of their 'priestly clothing', and why this happened.","model":"The 'priestly clothing' is the special status and mystique of the planner as an expert with unique knowledge, comparable to law or medicine. In the craft era and again in the systems era planners claimed to know what was best and politics was seen as passive or benign. Between 1965 and 1975 attacks from two sides removed this: from the right, pluralists such as Lindblom, Meyerson and Banfield and Altshuler showed that real decisions come from political bargaining and that planners had little power; from the left, advocacy planning (Davidoff) and the civil rights movement and urban riots fed distrust of expert top-down planning. As a result young planners saw themselves as 'barefoot doctors' for the inner-city poor, and planning lost its claim to unique expertise.","rubric":["Defines priestly clothing as the planner's expert status/mystique","Names the attack from the right (pluralists, e.g. Lindblom/Altshuler)","Names the attack from the left (e.g. Davidoff's advocacy, distrust of top-down planning)","States the consequence: loss of unique expertise (e.g. barefoot doctors, 1975 graduate)"]},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"Excerpt from Hall (2014), The City of Theory, reporting a critique of planning: 'And planning can never do more than modify some parameters of the land development process; it cannot change its intrinsic logic, and so cannot remove the contradiction between private accumulation and collective action. [...] Because traditional non-Marxian planning theory has ignored this essential basis of planning, so Marxian commentators argue, it is by definition vacuous: it seeks to define what planning ideally ought to be, devoid of all context; its function has been to depoliticize planning as an activity, and thus to legitimate it. [...] The reality, Marxists argue, is precisely the opposite: viewed objectively, planning theory is nothing other than a creation of the social forces that bring planning into existence.' (a) Which theoretical perspective is presented here? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the excerpt. (10)","model":"(a) Marxist (structuralist) political economy, as applied to planning in the 1970s by authors such as Scott and Roweis and Dear and Scott, and reported by Hall. (b) Argument 1, structure over agency: outcomes are determined by the capitalist land market, not by planners' choices. Planning 'can never do more than modify some parameters of the land development process; it cannot change its intrinsic logic, and so cannot remove the contradiction between private accumulation and collective action'. Planning operates inside a contradiction between private accumulation and collective needs that it can manage but not resolve, which is the base-superstructure logic: the economic base sets the limits of state action. Argument 2, theory as ideology: conventional planning theory is 'by definition vacuous', and 'its function has been to depoliticize planning as an activity, and thus to legitimate it'. Ideas do not drive reality; 'planning theory is nothing other than a creation of the social forces that bring planning into existence'. This is historical materialism: ideas belong to the superstructure and legitimate capitalist property relations. Hall himself accepts this as 'a disturbing body of coherent criticism' but calls its logic 'strangely quietist'. On the course framework this is a structural explanation: planners' agency is minimal, and change could only come from transforming the economic base rather than from better planning theory.","rubric":["(a) Names Marxist / structuralist political economy (not Hall's own view)","Argument 1 quotes the 'intrinsic logic' or 'contradiction between private accumulation and collective action' line and explains structural limits on planning","Argument 2 quotes 'vacuous', 'depoliticize ... legitimate' or 'creation of the social forces' and explains theory as ideology/superstructure","Links to course concepts (structure vs agency, base and superstructure)","Quotes are verbatim from the excerpt"]},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare the view of knowledge and theory of Hall's 1960s systems planners with Rydin's (2021) view of theory.","model":"The systems planners of the 1960s saw planning as 'a science, not an art'. Drawing on location theory, logical positivism (testing general hypotheses about spatial distributions) and cybernetics, they treated the city as a system that could be modelled, forecast, monitored and controlled. Knowledge was universal and monist: one scientific, quantitative form of expertise held by the planner, and the lecture places this period on the positivist side. Rydin (2021) rejects precisely this natural-science model of theory as 'a law, connecting variables together in a systematic way'. Following Flyvbjerg et al., she argues that the social world is complex and subject to a double hermeneutic. For her, theory is 'a heuristic aid to understanding a situation, a way to try to know what is essentially unknowable in its completeness': partial, narrative and value-laden, and the researcher must choose among contested theories. Hall's own account supports Rydin's position: he shows that cities had many contradictory objectives, probabilistic relations and an urban system inside its own system of control, so that by 1975 even Britton Harris doubted optimising methods. The systems view is universal and monist; Rydin's is situated and pluralist without being relativist.","rubric":["Characterises the systems view: science, modelling/control, positivist, universal and monist","Characterises Rydin: heuristic, partial, value-laden; rejects law-like theory (complexity, double hermeneutic)","Places both on the knowledge dimension correctly","Uses Hall's reasons for the failure of the systems model as a link","At least one accurate quote or close paraphrase from each text"]},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"position","pts":10,"q":"Use Hall's three graduate planners (1955, 1965, 1975) to show how the dominant view of knowledge and expertise in planning changed. Place each on the course's knowledge dimension (universal vs situated; monist vs pluralist) and justify.","model":"Hall's caricature: in 1955 the new graduate sat at the drawing board drawing land uses, in 1965 analysed computer output of traffic patterns, and in 1975 talked late into the night with community groups. The 1955 planner is a design craftsman: knowledge is the expert's intuition and judgement derived from architecture and engineering, goals implicit and drawn from his 'expert' values. It is monist (only the expert knows), and claimed to be general, though based on personal skill rather than science. The 1965 planner is a systems scientist: knowledge is quantitative, model-based and claimed to be objective, 'a science, not an art'; this is clearly universal and monist (positivist). The 1975 planner has lost the 'priestly clothing': knowledge is dispersed among groups and coloured by power and interests (Lindblom: every knower is limited; Davidoff: whose knowledge is heard?), so expertise shifts towards situated and pluralist knowledge, with the planner as advocate or facilitator. The direction is therefore from monist expert knowledge towards pluralism, which later becomes the communicative turn.","rubric":["Correctly describes the three graduates","Places 1955 as monist expert craft knowledge","Places 1965 as universal and monist / positivist","Places 1975 as more situated and pluralist, with a reason (pluralist critique, advocacy, loss of priestly clothing)","Identifies the overall direction of change"]},
/* ---- marx ---- */
{"t":"marx","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain what Marx means by 'species-being' and how estranged labour inverts it.","model":"Species-being is Marx's term for what makes humans human: they treat their own species and nature as objects of consciousness and will, and they produce consciously and freely. 'Conscious life activity distinguishes man immediately from animal life activity': an animal produces only under immediate physical need, whereas man 'produces even when he is free from physical need and only truly produces in freedom therefrom'. Estranged labour reverses this relation. Work becomes forced and external, a mere means to survive, so 'estranged labor makes man's species-life a means to his physical existence'. The worker then feels free only in animal functions such as eating and drinking, and in his human functions feels like an animal.","rubric":["Defines species-being as conscious, free, purposeful (universal) activity","Contrasts humans with animals (consciousness; producing free from need)","States the inversion: species-life becomes a means to physical existence","Mentions the human/animal reversal or labour as forced/external"]},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"An article on the Dutch housing crisis argues: 'Rising rents and overcrowding are not accidents or market failures that better regulation can fix. They follow from a system in which land and homes are owned privately and produced for profit. Tenants pay ever more for homes that others own, and every rise in property values enriches owners while making tenants poorer. Raising rent allowances only means paying the landlord more on the tenant's behalf. Real change requires taking housing out of the market altogether.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this passage? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the passage. (10)","model":"(a) Marxist political economy (historical materialism), in the planning translation of Madden and Marcuse (2016). (b) Argument 1, structure rather than failure or agency: the passage says the problems 'are not accidents or market failures that better regulation can fix. They follow from a system in which land and homes are owned privately and produced for profit.' Outcomes are explained by the base, ownership of the means of production (here land and housing), not by individual choices or correctable distortions; this mirrors Madden and Marcuse's claim that overcrowding and unaffordability are 'how the market works'. Argument 2, outcome fixes within the system cannot help, only structural change can: 'Raising rent allowances only means paying the landlord more on the tenant's behalf. Real change requires taking housing out of the market altogether.' This parallels Marx's argument that 'an enforced increase of wages' would be 'nothing but better payment for the slave', and his conclusion that emancipation requires ending private property; for housing, this means decommodification (public, cooperative ownership). The sentence 'every rise in property values enriches owners while making tenants poorer' also echoes Marx's starting fact that the worker becomes poorer the more wealth he produces. On the course framework this places the passage at the structural end and on the side of deliberate transformation of the base, rather than incremental reform of the existing housing market.","rubric":["(a) Names Marxist political economy / historical materialism (Madden and Marcuse a plus)","Argument 1 quotes the 'not accidents or market failures' or 'owned privately and produced for profit' line and explains structural causation","Argument 2 quotes the allowances or 'out of the market' line and links it to Marx's wage argument or decommodification","Accurate use of course concepts (base, private property, commodification)","Quotes are verbatim from the passage"]},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare Marx (1844) and Hayek on private property and freedom, and explain what each implies for the role of the planner.","model":"For Marx, private property is not a neutral precondition of freedom but 'the product, the result, the necessary consequence, of alienated labor'. Under private property and wage labour the worker's labour is 'not voluntary, but coerced; it is forced labor', and the more he produces, the poorer he becomes. Formal freedom to sell one's labour is therefore servitude, and even higher wages would be 'better payment for the slave'. Freedom requires abolishing private property, expressed politically as the emancipation of the workers. Hayek reasons the other way round: private property and competition under general rules disperse economic power and allow individuals to use their own knowledge; free disposal over property is a condition for freedom and democracy, while central control of the economy concentrates power. For planning, the HC4 lecture draws the contrast: Marx's commitment is material equality, so the progressive planner plans for the working class and the poor through decommodified models (Madden and Marcuse); Hayek's commitment is liberty and equal rights, so the planner sets general framework rules rather than designing outcomes. In Buitelaar's terms, Hayek founds the Free City, while Marx is closest to the Equal City.","rubric":["States Marx's view: private property as product of estranged labour; formal freedom as forced labour/servitude","States Hayek's view: private property/competition as a condition of freedom; dispersed power and knowledge","Draws the planning implications for each (decommodification/redistribution vs framework rules)","Names the contrasting normative commitments (material equality vs liberty/equal rights)","At least one accurate quote from Marx"]},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"position","pts":10,"q":"Place Marx, as presented in 'Estranged Labour' and the HC4 lecture, on the structure vs agency dimension and on the reproduction vs transformation and deliberate vs emergent dimensions. Justify each placement, and distinguish what comes from the text and what from the lecture.","model":"Structure: in the text the worker's misery is not a matter of bad choices but of the structure of private property and wage labour; even his experience of his own activity and of other people is shaped by his position as a worker. The lecture makes this explicit: the base (means and relations of production) shapes the superstructure, and humans are 'subject to rather than actors of' broad change, a deterministic view. Reproduction and transformation: the text explains reproduction, since 'the worker becomes all the poorer the more wealth he produces' and his labour strengthens the alien power of capital, but it aims at transformation: emancipation from private property, expressed as the emancipation of the workers. Deliberate vs emergent: change is driven by structural contradictions (the dialectic of owners and disowned), yet the political programme, revolution and the passage from capitalist to socialist to stateless society (Lenin), is a deliberate project; this programme comes from the lecture's framing, not from the 1844 text. For planning, the deliberate route appears as the progressive planner's decommodified housing, though Hall warns that Marxist logic can become 'quietist' for planners.","rubric":["Places Marx at the structure end with text-based and lecture-based support","Explains reproduction (worker poorer as wealth grows) and aim of transformation","Addresses deliberate vs emergent with nuance (structural contradictions vs deliberate political project)","Distinguishes the 1844 text from the lecture's framing (e.g. base/superstructure, Lenin, revolution)","At least one accurate quote"]}
];

const NQ_1=[
/* ---- fw ---- */
{"t":"fw","fmt":"recall","q":"In Rydin's Table 1.1, what does political economy (ch. 7) say the governmental approach neglects?","o":["The cultural dimension of organisations, including path dependency","The structural, particularly economic, forces that shape government action","The involvement of stakeholders in both policy formulation and implementation","The self-interest of state actors and the prevalence of state failure"],"a":1,"e":"Political economy's critique is that the governmental approach neglects structural, especially economic, forces. Culture and path dependency is the new institutionalist critique, stakeholder involvement the governance critique, and state failure the rational choice critique."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"example","q":"Rydin notes that data on housebuilding rates read differently from a historical view, a focus on housebuilders' behaviour or a record of planners' decisions. What does this example show?","o":["The same data can support different narratives, so an explicit theory is needed","Housebuilding data are too unreliable to be used in planning research at all","Planners' decisions always explain housebuilding rates better than market data","Historical research is the only neutral way to interpret housebuilding statistics"],"a":0,"e":"The example illustrates that data never prove a finding unequivocally: they must be interpreted into a narrative, each narrative implies value judgements, and an explicit theoretical perspective helps build the most persuasive one. It says nothing about data being unusable."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"inverse","q":"Which of these is one of the four questions of Flyvbjerg et al.'s phronetic research?","o":["Which law best connects the key variables of this case?","Who gains and who loses, and by which mechanisms of power?","Which instrument maximises the total welfare of the whole city?","Which data should be gathered so the facts can speak?"],"a":1,"e":"This is the second of Flyvbjerg et al.'s four questions (where are we going; who gains and who loses, by which mechanisms of power; is this desirable; what should we do). Searching for laws linking variables is the natural-science model that phronesis rejects, and letting facts speak is empiricism."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"define","q":"What does Latour's term 'modernist settlement', cited by Rydin, refer to?","o":["The post-war consensus that planning is a rational, expert-led activity of the state","The settlement between analytic and normative theory in planning research","The modernist design tradition of fixed end-state plans for new towns","The empiricist view that the world is out there and reveals itself through data"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin uses the term for empiricism: the belief that 'the facts can be allowed to speak for themselves' and the world only needs to be looked at to reveal itself. The 'post-war consensus' option describes the governmental model, a different idea."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"contrast","q":"Rydin shows three small shifts in defining planning. Which definition is the most politicised one?","o":["A form of decision-making that uses particular means to achieve specific stated goals","A state activity that orders the environment in the collective interest","An intervention that affects diverse interests, judged by who benefits from it","A future-oriented process through which actors try to achieve control"],"a":2,"e":"The shift runs from decision-making (instrumental, public administration) to state action in the collective interest, to intervention affecting diverse interests and systemic features of society. Only the last judges planning by which interests benefit most."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"apply","q":"In the HC1 Metropolis case, which consideration would most clearly favour the plan with 1,600 homes, 40% affordable and priority for low-income households?","o":["Whether the plan delivers the largest total number of homes for the city","Whether the landowner's right to build within existing rules is respected","Whether every low-income household ends up with an adequate, affordable home","Whether the plan avoids spending EUR 50 million of public money on new housing"],"a":2,"e":"A commitment to providing enough adequate, affordable housing for those who lack it (Buitelaar's Supportive City logic) points to the mixed plan. Total output and avoiding subsidy favour the 2,000-home market plan, and respecting the landowner's rights is the Free City criterion."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"recall","q":"The HC1 'glasses' slide shows what bundled together into 'Theory A', which then drives research design, questions and analysis?","o":["Structure, agency and institutions","Data, methods and research findings","Ontology, epistemology and values","Explanation, evaluation and prescription"],"a":2,"e":"The slide shows ontology + epistemology + values forming a theory, which works like a lens on research. Explanatory, evaluative and prescriptive are the kinds of claims a theory makes, a separate slide."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"not","q":"Rydin says four approaches 'look inward' at planning actors and organisations to improve planning. Which approach is NOT one of them?","o":["Political economy","Rational choice","New institutionalism","Governance theory"],"a":0,"e":"The inward-looking four are governmental, rational choice, new institutionalism and governance. Political economy looks beyond the state, seeking structural change in society to address socio-economic inequality."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"apply","q":"Practitioners complain that academic research is too critical and should tackle 'real, tangible issues'. What is one of Rydin's responses?","o":["Academics should drop theory so their findings can be applied directly by practitioners","Critique is always progressive, so practitioners' complaints can safely be set aside","Research should be judged only by whether planners actually adopt its recommendations in practice","Practice-led research carries its own values, e.g. choices between sustainability and growth"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin answers that research led by a practice agenda carries that agenda's value judgements: goals like sustainability, justice and economic development seem unassailable but planners must keep choosing between them. Her second response is that theory-informed research can support reflection, not that theory should be dropped."},
{"t":"fw","fmt":"contrast","q":"How does Rydin relate governance theory to collaborative planning theory?","o":["Governance is normative and collaborative planning analytic, and they share no common focus","Governance is analytic, collaborative planning normative, yet both centre on stakeholder interaction","Both are analytic theories from political science that explain urban policy mainly through market mechanisms","Both are normative theories that prescribe consensus, so they are the same framework"],"a":1,"e":"Governance theory explains policy through networks of stakeholders (analytic); collaborative planning wants more communicative action between them (normative). Their shared focus on stakeholder interaction shows that one framework can have analytic and normative sides."},
/* ---- buit ---- */
{"t":"buit","fmt":"define","q":"In Sen's bicycle example, as discussed by Buitelaar, what is the 'capability'?","o":["The bike itself, as a good that someone can own or buy","Being able to ride the bike, or to choose not to","The pleasure a person gets from riding the bike","The bike's feature that it can transport someone"],"a":1,"e":"Sen separates the commodity (the bike), its characteristic (it can transport someone), the capability (being able to ride it) and utility (the pleasure of riding). The capability approach asks what people are actually able to do and to be."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"recall","q":"What does hedonic price analysis do in Buitelaar's discussion of the Happy City?","o":["Measures subjective happiness directly through surveys, instead of using willingness to pay","Sets a sufficiency threshold for housing costs as a share of household income","Prices externalities like noise or green space by comparing exposed and unexposed homes","Calculates how unequally income is spread across the top and bottom deciles"],"a":2,"e":"Hedonic pricing infers the value of externalities (noise, flood risk, green space, heritage) from property price differences, extending willingness-to-pay logic. Measuring subjective happiness is an alternative some propose, which Buitelaar notes is still utilitarian."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"define","q":"What is a Pareto-optimal distribution, as used in the Happy City section?","o":["No one can be made better off without making someone else worse off","Everyone receives the same share of income, wealth and housing space","Everyone has at least enough to reach an agreed sufficiency threshold","The greatest number of people receives the greatest total happiness"],"a":0,"e":"In a perfect market equilibrium no further gains from trade exist: nobody can gain without someone losing. It is an efficiency criterion, not an equality or sufficiency criterion; Bentham's 'greatest happiness of the greatest number' is the Happy City's basis, not the Pareto definition."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"apply","q":"An investor legitimately buys and rents out dozens of homes and ends up owning far more than owner-occupiers. How does Nozick's entitlement theory, as used for the Free City, judge this?","o":["Unjust, because the resulting distribution of homes is very unequal","Unjust, unless every tenant still has an adequate, affordable home","Not unjust, as long as total housing welfare in the city is maximised","Not unjust, as long as acquisition and transfer were legitimate"],"a":3,"e":"Entitlement theory judges how holdings were acquired and transferred (exchange, gifts, purchase, inheritance are legitimate), not the resulting pattern. Buitelaar says eyebrows may be raised, but for liberalism this is no injustice."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT among Moroni's 'spatial primary goods' in the Supportive City section?","o":["Adequate and affordable housing for everyone","Accessibility to jobs from where people live","Equal average incomes across all neighbourhoods","Proximity to greenery and a safe living environment"],"a":2,"e":"Moroni's list is adequate and affordable housing, accessibility to jobs, proximity to greenery and a safe living environment, open to political adjustment. Equalising incomes across neighbourhoods is an Equal City aim, not a sufficiency good."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"recall","q":"Buitelaar calls Raworth's doughnut a version of limitarianism. What do its two boundaries represent?","o":["A statutory minimum wage as threshold and a maximum rent as ceiling on housing costs","A social foundation as sufficiency threshold and an ecological ceiling as wealth cap","An equality floor between deciles and a growth ceiling for the city economy","A threshold for room stress and a ceiling for the 30% housing ratio"],"a":1,"e":"Raworth's 'social foundation' provides the sufficiency threshold, while the 'ecological ceiling' constitutes a wealth limit. Limitarianism thus adds a ceiling to the Supportive City's floor."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"contrast","q":"Buitelaar distinguishes 'background justice' from 'allocative justice'. What is the difference?","o":["The basic structure of society (Rawls) vs who gets what in city-level decisions","Justice in acquiring property vs justice in transferring that property to other people","Fair procedures and recognition vs fair distribution of outcomes","Justice for current residents vs justice for future generations"],"a":0,"e":"Theories of justice often concern background justice, the basic structure of society; decisions at city level concern allocative justice, who gets what. Acquisition vs transfer are Nozick's two principles, and procedural/recognition justice is what Buitelaar sets aside."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"define","q":"Hayek, as used in the Free City section, distinguishes cosmos from taxis. What is cosmos?","o":["A designed order built according to a predetermined plan for the city","A legal framework that assigns equal rights to every resident of the city","A spontaneous order arising from free individuals without a final image","A balanced order in which every group receives an equal share of goods"],"a":2,"e":"Cosmos is spontaneous order, established through self-organisation by free individuals without a predetermined final image; taxis is created, designed order. Hayek holds that complex systems like cities can only come to order spontaneously."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"recall","q":"Why does Buitelaar see keeping 'justice' vague as a problem?","o":["It makes justice too narrow, so only one conception of it can ever be pursued","It forces planners to make explicit choices too early in the planning process","It lets the Free City dominate, because vague rules will always favour existing property rights","It creates superficial consensus, and hidden conflicts surface later less productively"],"a":3,"e":"Vagueness (after Sartori) avoids controversy and yields short-term, superficial consensus, while conflicts stay hidden and may resurface later in less productive ways. Defining justice too narrowly is the second, separate problem he identifies."},
{"t":"buit","fmt":"not","q":"Which policy does Buitelaar NOT link to the Happy City?","o":["Ex ante cost-benefit analysis","Promoting agglomeration economies","City marketing to attract the creative class","Social mixing of neighbourhoods"],"a":3,"e":"Social mixing is the Equal City's spatial policy. CBA is the Happy City's evaluation method, and agglomeration policies, including city marketing and attracting the creative class (Sager's list), are its typical urban policies."},
/* ---- hall ---- */
{"t":"hall","fmt":"recall","q":"In the Marxist debate on the state that Hall reports, what did Castells stress?","o":["Private productive investment, which the state underpins to keep capital accumulation going smoothly","Communicative action that can reveal rather than distort the workings of capitalism","Spontaneous order, which the state cannot replace with any designed distribution","Collective consumption, such as public housing and transport, that reproduces the labour force"],"a":3,"e":"Castells emphasised collective consumption (public housing, schools, transport), which reproduces labour and dampens class conflict. Lojkine stressed the state's support for private productive investment; communicative action is Forester's and spontaneous order Hayek's."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"example","q":"What did Altshuler's study of Minneapolis-St Paul show, according to Hall?","o":["The planner had no clout against a political machine that backed the highway engineers","The planner's computer models allowed him to outvote the highway engineers on most issues","Community groups successfully used advocacy planners to stop the region's freeway plans","The housing authority failed because it misread the local power structure of the city"],"a":0,"e":"Altshuler found the professional planner powerless against the political machine; the highway engineers won by stressing expertise and narrow goals. Misreading the power structure is Meyerson and Banfield's finding about the Chicago Housing Authority."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"define","q":"What did Bolan mean, as quoted by Hall, by calling systems planning old comprehensive planning 'dressed up in fancier garb'?","o":["Systems planning introduced a new aesthetic design language for comprehensive plans","Comprehensive plans became more expensive because they relied on large computer models","Systems jargon repackaged the old top-down comprehensive approach without changing it","Planners adopted professional dress codes to strengthen their claim to expert status"],"a":2,"e":"The left critique held that systems jargon masked the same expert, top-down comprehensive planning (and possibly unethical policies). The phrase is about substance being unchanged under new language, not about design or cost."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"recall","q":"According to Hall, where did the 1960s systems model of planning procedure largely come from?","o":["British new town design teams working under the 1946 New Towns Act","American transportation studies such as those of Detroit and Chicago","Advocacy planning practice with community groups in American inner cities","Habermas's critical theory as applied by Forester to planning practice"],"a":1,"e":"The model of setting goals, taking inventories, modelling, forecasting, evaluating alternatives and monitoring came from US transportation studies (Detroit 1955, Chicago 1956). Regional scientists later made the link between transport and land use two-way."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"contrast","q":"Sandercock contrasted planning's 'official story' with 'insurgent planning histories'. What is Hall's main objection?","o":["Insurgent histories ignore the role of women and minorities in planning","Official planning was in fact always malign, so Sandercock's contrast is far too mild","Community-based planning is simply advocacy planning under a new name","Redefining planning as something entirely different robs the word of meaning"],"a":3,"e":"Hall grants Sandercock a point but objects that redefining planning so broadly 'robs the word of all meaning' (Humpty Dumpty), and that hinting at a 'noir side' implies all official planning was secretly malign. He does not claim official planning was malign; that is what he criticises."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"apply","q":"The knowledge lecture illustrated one period of Hall's history with Berlage's Plan Zuid for Amsterdam, a detailed aesthetic blueprint. Which period does it illustrate?","o":["Planning as a craft before 1955: theory in planning but no theory of planning","Systems planning of the 1960s: the city as a system monitored and controlled","Advocacy planning of the late 1960s: planners working for unheard groups","Communicative planning of the 1990s: planning through dialogue and the search for consensus"],"a":0,"e":"A detailed, aesthetic end-state plan made by a designer is the pre-1955 craft tradition, derived from architecture and engineering. Systems planning replaced such blueprints with plans as trajectories of dynamic states that are monitored."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"recall","q":"How does Hall describe the position of academic theorists by the 1980s and 1990s, compared with the 1950s?","o":["Coaches down with the team, whereas they had been spectators in the grandstand","Spectators in the grandstand, whereas they had been coaches down with the team","Referees enforcing the rules, whereas they had been players on the pitch","Players on the pitch, whereas they had been referees enforcing the rules"],"a":1,"e":"Hall's metaphor: in the 1950s theorists were 'the coaches, down there with the team'; later they became spectators in the grandstand, as theory and practice drifted into divorce."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"not","q":"Which was NOT one of the intellectual sources of the 1960s quantitative and systems revolution in Hall's account?","o":["German location theory (von Thünen, Christaller)","Isard's regional science","Habermas's theory of communicative action","Wiener's cybernetics"],"a":2,"e":"Location theory, regional science and cybernetics fed the systems revolution. Habermas entered planning theory later, through Forester's attempt to escape the Marxist dilemma."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"define","q":"What new concept of planning did the systems era bring, according to Hall (drawing on Faludi and Webber)?","o":["A process in which programmes are adapted during implementation as information arrives","A blueprint with fixed objectives that the expert derives from his own values","A bargaining process in which groups adjust policy in small remedial steps","A communicative practice in which planners reveal rather than distort information to the public"],"a":0,"e":"Planning became a continuous process, adapted as new information arrives (Faludi), and 'a special way of deciding and acting' independent of what is planned (Webber). The blueprint describes the earlier craft era, and small remedial steps describe Lindblom."},
{"t":"hall","fmt":"contrast","q":"Hall cites Nigel Taylor's view of the theoretical shifts after the 1960s. What does Taylor argue?","o":["Each was a full Kuhnian paradigm shift that replaced the previous view entirely","Only the Marxist critique was a true paradigm shift, since it changed planning practice for good","Only the communicative turn was a paradigm shift, since practitioners adopted it","None was a true Kuhnian paradigm shift; they filled out a primitive post-war conception"],"a":3,"e":"Taylor argues that none of these shifts was a true paradigm shift in Kuhn's sense; they 'filled out' a primitive post-war conception of planning. This fits the lecture's image that theories do not replace one another cleanly."},
/* ---- marx ---- */
{"t":"marx","fmt":"recall","q":"How does Marx begin his analysis, in contrast to political economy's method?","o":["From a fictitious original condition of humanity, like the theologians' Fall","From an actual economic fact, rather than from a fictitious primordial condition","From the laws of competition, which he accepts as the true driving force","From Hegel's World Spirit, which he takes over as the motor driving all of history"],"a":1,"e":"Marx rejects explaining things by a fictitious 'primordial condition', which he compares to theologians explaining evil by the Fall: 'We proceed from an actual economic fact', namely that the worker becomes poorer as he produces more wealth."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"define","q":"What does Marx mean by the 'objectification' of labour?","o":["The legal process by which a worker becomes the owner of what he produces","The treatment of workers as mere objects of scientific study by classical political economy","Labour embodied in an object, which under estrangement appears as loss of the object","The conversion of nature into private property through enclosure of land"],"a":2,"e":"'The product of labor is labor which has been embodied in an object': in itself this is how humans realise themselves, but under the conditions of political economy objectification appears as loss of the object and bondage to it."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"example","q":"Marx compares the worker's relation to his product with religion. What is the point of the comparison?","o":["The more man puts into God, the less he retains in himself, just as with the product","Religion consoles the worker for estrangement, so it must be abolished first","Workers produce for God rather than for themselves, so labour becomes sacred","Like a church, the factory demands strict obedience to a fixed and timeless set of rules"],"a":0,"e":"Just as 'the more man puts into God, the less he retains in himself', the more the worker puts into the object, the less belongs to him and the more he falls under the dominion of his product. The comparison is about transfer of human powers to an alien power."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"recall","q":"Using political economy's own terms, into which two classes does Marx say society splits?","o":["Town dwellers and rural peasants","Skilled craftsmen and unskilled labourers","Property owners and propertyless workers","Political rulers and private citizens"],"a":2,"e":"Marx shows from political economy's premises that competition concentrates capital in few hands and that society divides into two classes: property owners and propertyless workers."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"inverse","q":"In the lecture's base and superstructure model, which of these belongs to the base?","o":["Ownership of land, factories and tools: the relations of production","Planning law and the zoning rules that regulate land use","The values and norms that make private property seem natural","Ideologies that present market outcomes as natural, fair and deserved"],"a":0,"e":"The base is material reality: the means and relations of production, i.e. who owns land, factories and tools. Law, norms and ideology belong to the superstructure, which in turn maintains the base."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"contrast","q":"According to the HC4 lecture, what did Marx keep from Hegel, and what did he replace?","o":["He kept the Spirit as motor of history and replaced the dialectic with gradual reform","He kept the dialectic and replaced the Spirit with material relations of production","He kept the idea of free individuals and replaced the state with spontaneous order","He kept private property and replaced class struggle with peaceful competition"],"a":1,"e":"Marx keeps Hegel's dialectic (history through opposing relations) but makes material relations, the opposition between owners and the disowned of the means of production, the motor of history instead of 'the Spirit'. This is historical materialism."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"define","q":"What does Marx mean by calling nature man's 'inorganic body'?","o":["Nature is a dead resource that has value only once labour transforms it","Human bodies are made of the same inorganic matter as the rest of nature","Nature belongs to whoever is first to enclose it and work it as property","Humans live from nature and must stay in continuous exchange with it"],"a":3,"e":"'Nature is man's inorganic body': humans physically live from nature and must remain in continuous interchange with it. Under estrangement the worker loses nature as the object of his own labour and as a direct means of subsistence."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"not","q":"Which statement is NOT part of the 'progressive planner' column (Madden and Marcuse) on the HC4 slide?","o":["Markets can work efficiently but are often distorted by land-use regulation","Markets do not deliver what they promise and produce material inequality","The planner ought to plan for the working class and the poor","Radically different models of planning are needed, such as decommodified housing"],"a":0,"e":"That markets can be efficient but are distorted by land-use regulation is the liberal planner position (Glaeser and Gyourko), who say planning should ensure a level field rather than limit supply. The other three statements are the progressive column."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"example","q":"Red Vienna's interwar municipal housing, such as the Karl-Marx-Hof, is used as an example on this page. What does it illustrate, and with what qualification?","o":["Spontaneous order in Hayek's sense, because tenants organised the estates themselves without any design","Decommodified housing (Madden and Marcuse), though as reform within capitalism, not abolition of private property","Estrangement from the product, because the workers who built the estates could never afford to live in them themselves","The Supportive City in its limitarian variant, because rents were capped for wealthier households"],"a":1,"e":"Low-rent municipal housing built by the city takes homes out of the market, which is decommodification; but it was a social-democratic reform inside a capitalist economy, not Marx's abolition of private property."},
{"t":"marx","fmt":"recall","q":"In 'Estranged Labour', how does Marx say the emancipation of society from private property is expressed politically?","o":["As a higher, enforced minimum wage","As equal civil rights for all citizens","As a planned welfare state for the poor","As the emancipation of the workers"],"a":3,"e":"Marx writes that 'the emancipation of society from private property, etc., from servitude, is expressed in the political form of the emancipation of the workers', because all human servitude is bound up in the worker's relation to production. Higher wages are explicitly rejected as a solution."}
];
